coat:
Unmoored, on what strange quest I willed to float;
Who wore a many-colored coat of dreams,
Thy gift, O Lord--I whom sun-dabbled streams

coats:
The blood-red wait for sweet summer heat,
Wrapped in bud-coats hairy and neat;
But the white buds swell, one day they will burst,

coasts:
I, writing thus, am still what men call young;
I have not so far left the coasts of life
To travel inland, that I cannot hear

coasting:
With jewels of the brave old year.
The coasting crowd upon the hill
With some new spirit seems to thrill;
